    <title>From Politics to Process: Evidence from Progressive Era Naturalizations -- by Abhay Aneja, Guo Xu</title>
    <link href="https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/?item=2026-10-22-72-from-politics-to-process-evidence"/>
    <link rel="related" href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35903#fromrss"/>
    <id>https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/item/2026-10-22-72-from-politics-to-process-evidence</id>
    <updated>2026-10-22T09:43:17.628650799+00:00</updated>
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;States that delegate governance to local officials face a trade-off between the benefits of local knowledge and the costs of biased administrators. Can imposing federal standards discipline bias and improve uniformity? We study this question by examining the introduction of federal standards to apply for citizenship under the 1906 Naturalization Act.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Source:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nber.org/papers/w35903#fromrss&quot;&gt;NBER Working Papers (National Bureau of Economic Research)&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;strong&gt;Tags:&lt;/strong&gt; #macroeconomics #econometrics #infosec&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
